Manii — Technical Overview
Version: 1.1 Platform: iOS (Expo / React Native) Companion to: Manii Product Requirements Document (v2) Last updated: July 2026
This document describes how Manii is built: the architecture, the major components, how they talk to each other, and the key technical decisions and risks. It deliberately excludes database tables, schemas, and security policies — those live in a separate data-model document.
What changed in v1.1 — decisions locked since v1.0: Suno is out of scope for v1; RevenueCat is confirmed; Journey memory uses a two-tier (Journey + global user) model; content generation is triggered through AI tool-calling into a human-confirmed modal, never by the model alone; quick-action buttons and context-aware suggestion chips are defined; generation runs as a background job that locks only its own conversation; and credit allowances/pack pricing scale by plan and billing period. Frequency and ambient audio are confirmed as cheap/free asset paths outside the credit system.

1. Purpose & Scope
The goal of this document is to give anyone working on Manii (or evaluating it) a clear mental model of the system before touching code. It covers the client app, the backend, the AI layer, the audio pipeline, subscriptions, and the credit system, and it flags the load-bearing decisions that shape everything downstream.
Two framing facts drive most of the architecture:
AI and audio synthesis cost real money per request. The whole system is designed so that expensive operations happen server-side, are metered, and can never be triggered for free or abused.
Generation is slow; conversation is fast. These are treated as two different systems with different models, different latency expectations, and different cost profiles.

2. Architectural Principles
The client is never trusted with secrets or cost decisions. No third-party API key (OpenAI, Claude, ElevenLabs) ever ships in the app. Every paid call is proxied through the backend, which authenticates the user, checks entitlements and credits, then makes the call.
Expensive work is asynchronous. Audio generation is a job, not a request/response. The client submits it and is notified when it's done, rather than holding a connection open.
A human tap always triggers spend. The AI can propose generating content, but it never spends a credit on its own — generation only fires after the user confirms in a modal (see §8).
State of record lives in the backend. Credits, entitlements, Journey memory, and generated-audio metadata are authoritative in the database, not on the device.
Fail closed on money. If credit balance or subscription status can't be verified, the action is denied rather than allowed.
The app should feel calm and instant even when the backend is slow. Optimistic UI, clear progress states, and local caching hide latency from the user.

