16. Key Technical Decisions & Risks
Resolved since v1.0:
✅ RevenueCat is the subscriptions/trial/entitlements backbone.
✅ Suno is out for v1; not a launch dependency.
✅ Memory is two-tier (Journey scope + global user scope), built with an extract → store → retrieve pattern.
✅ Generation is triggered by AI tool-calling into a human-confirmed modal — the model never spends credits alone.
✅ Concurrency follows the "deep research" model: only the active conversation's input locks; the rest of the app stays usable.
✅ Frequency = DSP/preloaded, ambient = licensed loops, both outside the credit system.
✅ Credit allowances and pack pricing scale by plan and billing period, config-driven.
Still open — resolve before the affected build phase:
Where long-form audio mixing runs. Edge functions may not handle mixing + looping for 60–120 min and 8-hour sessions within their limits. Decide between a dedicated worker service and a constrained edge-function approach before building long audio. (High impact.)
Conversation vs generation model selection. Pick the specific low-cost and high-quality models, keep them swappable via config, since cost testing may change the choice.
Exact credit-cost numbers and the allowance/pack matrix values — a business decision after cost testing. The mechanism is settled; the numbers aren't.
Memory summarisation specifics. How facts are extracted, scoped, and retrieved needs concrete definition in the data-model phase.

17. Suggested Build Sequencing
A pragmatic order that de-risks the hardest parts early without blocking on them:
Foundations — Expo app shell with the three tabs, Supabase auth, and the Home chat UI wired to the conversation AI through an edge function (no generation yet). Proves the secure-proxy pattern end to end.
Journeys & memory — creating/listing/renaming/archiving Journeys, plus the two-tier extract → store → retrieve memory loop that feeds context back into chat.
Generation trigger + pipeline (short audio first) — tool-calling → confirmation modal → script generation → ElevenLabs → simple assembly → playable card, capped at short lengths to sidestep the mixing/worker question initially.
Credits & subscriptions — RevenueCat integration, trial enforcement, entitlement gating, the atomic credit ledger, and the commitment-based allowance/pack matrix.
Long-form audio & looping — introduce the worker/mixing approach for 60 min → 8 hour sessions.
Ambient & frequency library — asset-based and DSP content types.
Polish — background playback, offline caching, sidebar search, suggestion chips, and the design language (dark-first, muted purples/mauves, warm whites, large spacing).
Sleep tab — remains a "Coming Soon" placeholder until post-launch.

End of Technical Overview. Database tables, schemas, and security policies are specified separately in the data-model document.

